BILL NUMBER: S7737

SPONSOR: HOYLMAN-SIGAL
 
TITLE OF BILL:

An act to amend the penal law, in relation to facilitating the prose-
cution of hate crimes; and to repeal subdivision 3 of section 240.30 of
the penal law

 
PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:

To modernize the offenses eligible for hate crime prosecution by updat-
ing the list of eligible offenses and by further delineating the umbrel-
la offense of aggravated harassment

 
SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:

Section 1 amends subdivision 3 of section 485.05 of the penal law to add
31 offenses as specified offenses eligible for hate crime prosecution.

These additional offenses include:

1. gang assault in the second degree;

2. gang assault in the first degree;

3. criminal obstruction of breathing or blood circulation;

4. aggravated murder;

5. murder in the first degree;

6. sexual misconduct;

7. rape in the third degree;

8. rape in the second degree;

9. criminal sexual act in the third degree;

10. criminal sexual act in the second degree;

11 forcible touching;

12 persistent sexual abuse;

13 sexual abuse in the third degree;

14 sexual abuse in the second degree;

15 aggravated sexual abuse in the fourth degree;

16 aggravated sexual abuse in the third degree;

17 labor trafficking;

18 aggravated labor trafficking;

19 making graffiti;
20 arson in the fifth degree;

21 jostling;

22 sex trafficking;

23 sex trafficking of a child;

24 falsely reporting an incident in the third degree;

25 falsely reporting an incident in the second degree;

26 falsely reporting an incident in the first degree;

27 endangering the welfare of a child;

28. criminal possession of a weapon in the fourth degree;

29. criminal possession of a weapon in the third degree;

30. criminal possession of a weapon in the second degree; and

31. criminal possession of a weapon in the first degree.

Section 2 amends Section 240.30 of the penal law to redefine the offense
of aggravated harassment in the second degree. It removes Section
240.30(3) (relating to unwelcome physical contact based upon belief or
perception about membership in a protected class)from its definition.

Section 3 amends Section 240.31 of the penal law to redefine the offense
of aggravated harassment in the first degree. It renames the current
offense of "aggravated harassment in the first degree" to "bias harass-
ment in the first degree." It modifies the prior offenses needed to
qualify for this offense for consistency with the rest of this act.

Section 4 adds a new section to the penal law, 240.30-a, which creates
the offense of bias harassment in the second degree. It is defined by
using the former Section 240.30(3), which relates to unwelcome physical
contact based upon belief or perception of membership in a protected
class.

Section 5 sets the effective date.

 
JUSTIFICATION:

The Hate Crimes Modernization Act seeks to expand the list of offenses
in the hate crimes statute to include categories of offenses such as
gang assault, certain sex crimes, human trafficking, graffiti, false
reporting, and criminal possession of a weapon.

These offenses can be bias-motivated and occur with unfortunate regular-
ity, but cannot be legally charged as hate crimes without amending the
current law. Hate crimes harm not just the victim, but the larger commu-
nity. It is important that this statute is amended to more effectively
remediate such crimes' pervasive harm.

New York has seen a dramatic increase in reported hate crimes since
2015. According to the New York State Division of Criminal Justice,
reported hate crimes increased by 55.8% in 2021 alone.' Offenses that
many New Yorkers would view as hate crimes - such as bias-motivated gang
assault, graffiti, falsely reporting an incident and others - are not
legally eligible to be charged as hate crimes under the existing hate
crimes statute.

The law should reflect a common understanding of what a hate crime is
and so that the law can protect people and property from the pervasive
effect of hate crimes. We can do this by updating the hate crimes stat-
ute to include specific criminal offenses that were initially excluded
from the legislation from over 20 years ago.

 
PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:

None.

 
FISCAL IMPLICATIONS:

TBD

 
EFFECTIVE DATE:
This act shall take effect immediately.
